# Keep working copies LF on every platform, regardless of core.autocrlf.
#
# Git for Windows sets core.autocrlf=true by default, which rewrites the whole
# tree to CRLF on `git checkout .` or `git reset --hard`. Those are exactly the
# commands used to abandon a bad refactor, so without this a revert arrives
# buried in 157 files of line-ending churn.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Batch files are the exception, and it is not cosmetic: cmd.exe seeks by byte
# offset when it executes a `goto`, and those offsets assume CRLF. Given LF-only
# endings the seek lands one byte late per line and drifts further the deeper
# into the file it goes, so the interpreter starts eating the first character of
# each command — `set` runs as `et`, `echo` as `cho`, then as `ho`. du.bat is
# entirely goto dispatch, so `du publish` failed partway through with a screen of
# "'et' is not recognized" and no indication that line endings were involved.
# The rule above had been quietly breaking every .bat in the repo on checkout.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# Binary assets: never touch these.
*.png binary
*.db  binary
